        "Long before anyone in a Hong Kong household is weighing IB against A Level, IGCSE has usually already set the pattern: nearly every private international and British-curriculum school here teaches Cambridge or Edexcel IGCSE to fourteen- to sixteen-year-olds as the fixed step before that later choice gets made. It is not a recent addition to the territory's education scene — a long-standing British and international school presence means the qualification has been taught here for decades, with results and teaching experience to match.",
        "Rather than one fixed diploma, a student collects individual IGCSE subject certificates, usually seven to ten of them across a couple of years. A good number of those subjects split into Core and Extended tiers, where Core caps how high a grade can go and Extended opens the door to the top marks — and because that tier call generally gets locked in roughly a year before the exam, it is not a decision worth making casually. Grades run A* through G at most Hong Kong schools, occasionally 9 through 1 for the newer UK-aligned syllabuses, and results come from two annual windows: the main May/June sitting and a second series in October/November that gives a genuine resit option HKDSE's single annual cycle simply doesn't have.",
